1. Introduction and Mathematical Problem Statement

Consider a colony of N humans and an AI task allocator forming a Cyber‑Physical‑Social System (CPSS). The state of the social system at time t is a vector x_t that captures the collective “configuration of meaning.” The central problem is to design an AI control policy \pi that guarantees two fundamental properties:

· (P1) Collective Coherence: Over any mission horizon T,
  \mathbb{P}\bigl[\forall h\;\lozenge_{\le T}\,\text{Belong}(h)\bigr] \ge 1-\delta,
  i.e., with probability at least 1-\delta every crew member experiences a sense of belonging at least once within T.
· (P2) Anti‑stagnation (Stochastic Permanence): The human purpose‑engagement metric X_t remains bounded away from zero in probability: for every \delta\in(0,1) there exists \varepsilon_\delta>0 such that
  \liminf_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{P}[X_t \ge \varepsilon_\delta] \ge 1-\delta.

The policy must simultaneously:

· never violate survival‑critical efficiency constraints,
· never coerce individuals into prescribed activities,
· be resilient to Goodhart’s law (gaming of the metric).

The proposed research develops a purely theoretical solution to this problem, synthesizing information geometry, stochastic analysis, optimal control, and formal verification. All results are presented as theorems with complete proofs.

