4. Affordance‑Based Optimal Control

4.1 Control as Environmental Affordance Design

To avoid the “totalitarian paradox”—that meaning cannot be prescribed—the AI does not assign tasks directly. Instead, the control u belongs to a compact set \mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{R}^m parametrising environmental modifications (resource placement, accessibility of art supplies, layout of common spaces). Given state x and affordance u, each human h voluntarily initiates a meaning‑bearing action a with probability p_h(a\mid x,u). The expected colony‑scale dynamics then take the form of a controlled jump‑diffusion on a compact state manifold \mathcal{M} (a sublevel set of F):

dx_t = f(x_t,u_t)\,dt + \Sigma(x_t)\,d\mathbf{W}_t + \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \gamma(x_{t-},u_t,z)\,\tilde{N}(dt,dz), \tag{1}

where \mathbf{W}_t is an m-dimensional Brownian motion, \tilde{N} a compensated Poisson random measure with finite Lévy measure \nu.

Assumption H1.

· f:\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{U}\to\mathbb{R}^n is continuous, Lipschitz in x uniformly in u: |f(x,u)-f(y,u)|\le L_f|x-y|, and |f(x,u)|\le C_f(1+|x|).
· \Sigma:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times m} is Lipschitz.
· \gamma(x,u,z) is measurable, |\gamma|\le C_\gamma(1+|x|)\rho(z), |\gamma(x,u,z)-\gamma(y,u,z)|\le L_\gamma|x-y|\rho(z) with \rho(z)=1\wedge|z| and \int\rho^2(z)\nu(dz)<\infty.
· f(x,u)\in T_x\mathcal{M} and x+\gamma(x,u,z)\in\mathcal{M} for \nu-a.e. z, so that the process stays on \mathcal{M}.

Under H1, for any admissible control u_\cdot, (1) has a unique strong càdlàg solution with finite second moments.

The instantaneous reward R(x,u) comprises a penalty for low belonging, a bonus for inter‑community bridging, a penalty for overt/covert discrepancy, and an efficiency cost C_{\text{eff}}(u):

R(x,u) = \sum_{i=1}^4 \omega_i \Phi_i(x) - C_{\text{eff}}(u),

with \omega_i>0 and R bounded and continuous.

Definition 4.1 (Agency safety). A policy \pi is agency‑safe if it never forces an individual into a prescribed activity; it only modifies environmental affordances. Survival‑critical tasks are allocated by a separate, minimal‑burden fairness mechanism outside the scope of this controller.

4.2 The Affordance HJB Equation

The value function

V(x,t) = \sup_{\{u_s\}_{s\ge t}} \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \int_t^T e^{-\rho(s-t)} R(x_s,u_s)\,ds + \Psi(x_T) \;\middle|\; x_t = x \right]

formally satisfies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman integro‑differential equation

\begin{aligned}
0 = \max_{u\in\mathcal{U}} \Bigl\{ 
&R(x,u) - \rho V + \nabla V\cdot f(x,u) + \frac{1}{2}\operatorname{Tr}\bigl(\Sigma\Sigma^{\!\top}\nabla^2 V\bigr) \\
&+ \int \bigl[V(x+\gamma(x,u,z)) - V(x) - \nabla V\!\cdot\!\gamma(x,u,z)\bigr]\nu(dz) \Bigr\},
\end{aligned} \tag{2}

with terminal condition V(x,T)=\Psi(x).

4.3 Viscosity Solution: Existence and Uniqueness

Since V is generally not smooth, we employ the theory of viscosity solutions for integro‑differential equations. Let \mathcal{L}^u denote the generator associated with (1). A continuous function V is a viscosity subsolution (resp. supersolution) of (2) if for any smooth test function \varphi touching V from above (resp. below) at (x_0,t_0),

-\varphi_t(x_0,t_0) + \rho V(x_0,t_0) - \sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\Bigl\{ R(x_0,u) + \mathcal{L}^u\varphi(x_0,t_0) \Bigr\} \le 0 \quad (\text{resp. } \ge 0).

Theorem 4.2 (Existence and uniqueness of viscosity solution).
Under H1 and bounded continuous R,\Psi, the HJB equation (2) admits a unique bounded continuous viscosity solution which coincides with the value function V.

Proof (Sketch).
Continuity of V follows from Lipschitz estimates for the controlled SDE and the boundedness of rewards.
Viscosity property is verified via the dynamic programming principle (DPP) and Itô’s formula for test functions; a standard contradiction argument using the DPP shows that V is a viscosity solution.
Comparison principle (uniqueness): assume \underline{V} is a subsolution and \bar{V} a supersolution with \underline{V}(\cdot,T)\le\bar{V}(\cdot,T). Define a doubled variable functional

\Phi_{\alpha}(x,y,t) = \underline{V}(x,t) - \bar{V}(y,t) - \frac{\alpha}{2}|x-y|^2 - \frac{\epsilon}{2}(|x|^2+|y|^2).

Suppose \sup(\underline{V}-\bar{V}) = m > 0. For large \alpha, the maximum of \Phi_\alpha converges to a point (x_0,x_0,t_0). Applying Ishii’s lemma for non‑local operators and using the Lipschitz properties of the coefficients, one obtains a contradiction as \alpha\to\infty unless m\le 0. The non‑local terms are handled by exploiting the Lipschitz continuity of \gamma and the penalization terms. Thus \underline{V}\le\bar{V}, establishing uniqueness.
Existence follows from Perron’s method: the value function is shown to be a viscosity solution; uniqueness guarantees it is the only one. ∎

4.4 Switched Heuristic Policy with Guarantees

Solving the full HJB online is intractable. We construct a switched heuristic policy: partition the compact state manifold \mathcal{M} into a finite collection of regions \{\mathcal{R}_k\}_{k=1}^K with diameter \le\delta. In each region, choose an affine (or constant) control u = \Pi_k(x) such that the Hamiltonian error is minimal.

Theorem 4.3 (ε‑optimality with bounded efficiency loss).
Under H1 and Lipschitz continuity of R in x, there exists a finite partition and affine controllers such that the switched policy \pi_S satisfies

V^{\pi_S}(x,0) \ge V^*(x,0) - \epsilon,

with \epsilon = O(\delta), while the expected discounted efficiency loss is at most \Delta_{\max}:= \max_{u\in\mathcal{U}}|C_{\text{eff}}(u)-C_{\text{eff}}(u_{\text{AI}})| < \infty. Agency safety is maintained because the policy only modulates the environment.

Proof (Sketch).
Mollify the value function to obtain a C^2 approximation V^\epsilon. Because V^* is semiconcave (a consequence of the control problem’s structure), the Hamiltonian evaluated at the optimal control is uniformly Lipschitz in x. Thus, within each region, the difference between the optimal Hamiltonian at the center x_k and at any point x is bounded by L_{H^*}\delta. The suboptimality of the switched policy accumulates at a rate proportional to this error, leading to \epsilon \le C\delta/\rho via Grönwall’s inequality. The efficiency loss bound is immediate from compactness of \mathcal{U} and the fact that the affordance policy never enforces full automation. ∎

