5. Formal Verification of Belonging and Identity

5.1 PCTL Specifications

The sociological requirement “every individual regularly feels a sense of belonging” is formalised in Probabilistic Computation Tree Logic (PCTL). For a human h,

\Phi_{\text{belong}}(h) \equiv \mathbb{P}_{\ge 1-\delta}\!\bigl[ \lozenge^{\le T} (\text{BelongState}(h)) \bigr],

where \text{BelongState}(h) holds if h has participated in at least one meaningful community interaction within the last W time units and has not been isolated for more than I_{\max} consecutive hours. The colony‑level safety property is

\Phi_{\text{colony}} \equiv \bigwedge_{h=1}^{N} \Phi_{\text{belong}}(h) \;\land\; \mathbb{P}_{\ge 1-\varepsilon}\!\bigl[ \Box^{\le T_{\text{mission}}} \text{SafetyCriticalTasksCompleted} \bigr].

5.2 Predicate Abstraction to an Interval MDP

Direct verification on the continuous state space is intractable. We develop a predicate abstraction: an abstraction map \alpha:\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{S}, where \mathcal{S} is a finite set of abstract states corresponding to a partition of \mathcal{M} that respects the atomic predicates in \Phi_{\text{colony}}. By time‑discretizing the controlled diffusion with step \Delta>0, we obtain a Markov kernel P^{\text{conc}}(x,u,\cdot). The abstraction induces an interval MDP (IMDP) with transition bounds

\check{P}_u(s,s') = \inf_{x\in\alpha^{-1}(s)} P^{\text{conc}}(x,u,\alpha^{-1}(s')), \quad
\hat{P}_u(s,s') = \sup_{x\in\alpha^{-1}(s)} P^{\text{conc}}(x,u,\alpha^{-1}(s')).

Definition 5.1 (Probabilistic simulation). \alpha is a probabilistic simulation if for every concrete state x and abstract state s=\alpha(x) there exists a weight function \Delta such that for each control u,

\check{P}_u(s,\cdot) \;\le\; (\alpha_* P^{\text{conc}}(x,\cdot)) \;\le\; \hat{P}_u(s,\cdot)

in the sense of stochastic dominance.

Lemma 5.2 (Soundness of abstraction).
Assume the boundaries of all atomic predicates are Lipschitz with respect to the geodesic distance on \mathcal{M} (Assumption A2), and the concrete transition kernel is weakly continuous (Feller property, a consequence of H1). If \alpha is a probabilistic simulation, then for any PCTL formula \psi without nested probabilistic operators,

\mathcal{I} \models_{lower} \psi \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \text{concrete system satisfies } \psi,

where lower satisfaction means the lower bound over all interval resolutions exceeds the required probability.

Proof (Sketch). By structural induction on \psi. For the base case of atomic propositions, preservation holds by construction of the partition. For \mathbb{P}_{\ge 1-\delta}[\lozenge^{\le T}\Phi], suppose the IMDP satisfies the lower bound. The probabilistic simulation guarantees that for any concrete path starting at x, one can couple it with a path in the IMDP such that the probability of eventually reaching an accepting state is at least as large as the IMDP’s lower bound. The coupling is built stepwise using the dominance relations. As the partition diameter tends to zero, Assumption A2 and uniform continuity of the kernel force the interval widths to vanish, ensuring the abstraction can be refined to arbitrary precision. ∎

5.3 Lean 4 Mechanisation

A central contribution of this dissertation is the formal verification of the finite‑state abstraction and PCTL model checking inside the Lean 4 proof assistant. This mechanisation will include:

· A formal definition of interval Markov chains and PCTL semantics.
· A proof that the abstraction map \alpha is a probabilistic simulation for a 50‑agent model under a counting abstraction (exploiting agent exchangeability).
· A certified model‑checking algorithm that verifies \Phi_{\text{colony}} on the reduced state space.
· A CEGAR driver that automatically refines the partition when a spurious counterexample is detected, with termination guaranteed by Assumption A2.

The continuous SDE and HJB parts remain in the analytical domain, providing a clean separation of proof obligations.

