Appendix A: Year 1 – Information Geometry and Stochastic Anti‑Stagnation

A.1 Concrete Cultural Manifold: The Dirichlet Interaction Model

We illustrate the abstract construction of Section 2.1 with a fully explicit example. Suppose the colony’s voluntary interactions are categorized into K types (e.g., creative, technical, social). Each day, M interaction events are observed, yielding a count vector n=(n_1,\dots,n_K) with \sum n_k = M. We model n as multinomial with probability vector \theta\in\Theta = \Delta^{K-1}_+ (the open simplex). The likelihood is

p(n\mid\theta) = \frac{M!}{\prod_k n_k!} \prod_{k=1}^K \theta_k^{n_k}.

The Fisher information metric on \Theta is well known:

g_{ij}(\theta) = M\left( \frac{\delta_{ij}}{\theta_i} + \frac{1}{\theta_K} \right), \qquad i,j = 1,\dots,K-1.

Geodesics are straight lines in the \sqrt{\theta} representation (the sphere). The cultural free energy is taken as

F(\theta) = U(\theta) - T\,S(\theta), \quad U(\theta) = \sum_{i<j} w_{ij}\theta_i\theta_j \text{ (friction)}, \quad S(\theta) = -\sum_k \theta_k\log\theta_k \text{ (entropy)}.

Properness of F follows because \Theta is bounded and F blows up at the boundary (\theta_k\to0). This gives a concrete, low‑dimensional Riemannian manifold fulfilling Assumption A1.

A.2 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Global Existence of the Meaning Flow)

Let (\mathcal{M},g) be a smooth Riemannian manifold, F\in C^2(\mathcal{M}) proper and bounded below. For any \theta_0\in\mathcal{M}, consider the initial value problem

\dot\theta(t) = -\nabla_g F(\theta(t)), \quad \theta(0)=\theta_0. \tag{A.1}

Local existence: In a coordinate chart, the right‑hand side is smooth in \theta, hence locally Lipschitz. By the Picard–Lindelöf theorem, there exists a unique C^1 solution on a maximal interval [0,\tau_{\max}).

Global extension: Compute the energy dissipation:

\frac{d}{dt}F(\theta(t)) = \langle\nabla_g F,\dot\theta\rangle_g = -\|\nabla_g F\|_g^2 \le 0.

Thus F(\theta(t)) \le F(\theta_0) for all t\in[0,\tau_{\max}). Because F is proper, the sublevel set \mathcal{K} = \{\theta : F(\theta) \le F(\theta_0)\} is compact. The solution remains in \mathcal{K}, which is a compact subset of \mathcal{M}. By the escape lemma for ODEs on manifolds, a solution that stays within a compact set can be extended for all t\ge0. Hence \tau_{\max}=+\infty. \square

Note: Properness of F is the essential condition preventing finite‑time blow‑up or hitting the boundary of \Theta. In the Dirichlet example, it corresponds to the fact that vanishing of any interaction type is penalised by an infinite entropy barrier.

A.3 Dual‑Channel Metric: Formal Definition and Goodhart Resilience

Define the overt channel C_1 as the vector of aggregate modularity and voluntarily initiated task counts. The covert channel C_2 is an aggregated score \zeta_t derived from zero‑knowledge proofs of meaningfulness perception. Let \hat{p}_{\text{overt},t} and \hat{p}_{\text{covert},t} be the empirical distributions of the two channels over a sliding window. The purpose‑engagement metric is

X_t = \Phi(\text{Modularity}_t, \zeta_t) - \lambda D_{\text{KL}}(\hat{p}_{\text{overt},t} \| \hat{p}_{\text{covert},t}),

where \Phi(a,b)=a^\alpha b^\beta and D_{\text{KL}} is the Kullback–Leibler divergence. The divergence penalty ensures resilience: any strategy that inflates overt activity without corresponding covert satisfaction increases D_{\text{KL}} and reduces X_t. In the cryptographic protocol, non‑interactive zero‑knowledge proofs guarantee that the aggregated \zeta_t is verifiable without revealing individual answers; the discrepancy between channels can be detected with high probability (details deferred to a separate cryptographic analysis).

A.4 Construction of the Bounded AI Saturation Process Y_t

We define Y_t = \sigma(Z_t) where \sigma(z)=1/(1+e^{-z}) is the logistic function, and Z_t solves the SDE

dZ_t = b(Z_t, X_t)\,dt + \nu\,\sigma(Z_t)(1-\sigma(Z_t))\,dB_t + \tilde\xi\,dN_t^{(Y)},

b(z,x) = \delta(1-2\sigma(z)) + \zeta x(1-\sigma(z)),

with \delta,\nu,\zeta>0, B_t a standard Brownian motion, N_t^{(Y)} a Poisson process with intensity \lambda_Y\ge0, and \tilde\xi a bounded random variable (jump size). The coefficients are globally bounded: |\sigma(z)(1-\sigma(z))|\le 1/4, |\delta(1-2\sigma(z))|\le\delta, and |b(z,x)|\le \delta + \zeta\|x\|_\infty provided X_t is a.s. bounded (which follows from Theorem 3.1). Therefore, by standard SDE theory (e.g., Applebaum, Lévy Processes and Stochastic Calculus, Thm. 6.4.5), the equation has a unique strong solution without finite‑time explosion. Since \sigma:\mathbb{R}\to(0,1) is a smooth bijection, Y_t = \sigma(Z_t) remains strictly in (0,1) for all t almost surely. Consequently,

Y_t \in [0,1] \quad \text{a.s. for all } t,

a deterministic bound that will be crucial in the Lyapunov analysis. This construction avoids any boundary classification arguments.

A.5 Full Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Stochastic Permanence)

We consider the system

\begin{aligned}
dX_t &= X_{t-}\bigl[(\alpha - \beta Y_t - \gamma X_t)\,dt + \sigma\,dW_t\bigr] + X_{t-}\,\kappa\,dN_t^{(X)},\\
dY_t &= \text{as above, with } Y_t\in[0,1]\text{ a.s.},
\end{aligned}

with independent Brownian motions W_t and B_t, Poisson process N_t^{(X)} with intensity \lambda_X, jump multiplier \kappa>-1 a.s., and parameters \alpha,\beta,\gamma,\sigma>0. Initial condition X_0>0.

Assumptions:

· \mathbb{E}[\log(1+\kappa)]<\infty;
· \mathbb{E}[(1+\kappa)^p]<\infty for some p>0 (to be chosen);
· Condition (C): \delta_0 := \alpha - \frac{\sigma^2}{2} - \beta > 0.

Part (i): Almost‑sure positivity.
Between jumps, X_t follows the geometric SDE

dX_t = X_t\bigl[(\alpha - \beta Y_t - \gamma X_t)\,dt + \sigma\,dW_t\bigr].

The solution can be written explicitly using the stochastic exponential: for any finite interval, it is strictly positive because the drift and diffusion are such that the process never hits zero (the geometric Brownian motion part, even with time‑dependent drift, remains positive). At a jump time \tau, X_\tau = X_{\tau-}(1+\kappa) > 0 because 1+\kappa>0 a.s. Hence by induction over jump times, X_t>0 for all t a.s. \square

Part (ii): Stochastic permanence.
We prove that for any \delta\in(0,1) there exists \varepsilon_\delta>0 such that \liminf_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{P}(X_t\ge\varepsilon_\delta)\ge 1-\delta.

The proof uses a combined Lyapunov function

U(x) = x^{-\theta} + x^{p},

with small \theta\in(0,1) and p>0 chosen later. Because Y_t is bounded, we treat the generator \mathcal{L} acting on functions of x alone (the dynamics of Y_t are independent of X_t except through the drift of Z_t, but we will bound the generator uniformly in y\in[0,1]).

The infinitesimal generator for the jump‑diffusion X_t (with y treated as a parameter) applied to a smooth function \varphi(x) is

\mathcal{L}\varphi(x,y) = \varphi'(x) x(\alpha - \beta y - \gamma x) + \tfrac12 \varphi''(x) \sigma^2 x^2 + \lambda_X \bigl(\mathbb{E}[\varphi(x(1+\kappa))] - \varphi(x)\bigr).

Step 1: Generator of x^{-\theta}.
For \varphi(x)=x^{-\theta}:

\varphi'(x) = -\theta x^{-\theta-1}, \qquad \varphi''(x) = \theta(\theta+1) x^{-\theta-2}.

Thus

\mathcal{L}(x^{-\theta}) = -\theta x^{-\theta} (\alpha - \beta y - \gamma x) + \tfrac12 \theta(\theta+1) x^{-\theta} \sigma^2 + \lambda_X x^{-\theta}\bigl(\mathbb{E}[(1+\kappa)^{-\theta}] - 1\bigr).

Grouping terms:

\mathcal{L}(x^{-\theta}) = -\theta x^{-\theta}\Bigl( \alpha - \beta y - \frac{\theta+1}{2}\sigma^2 \Bigr) + \theta\gamma x^{1-\theta} + \lambda_X x^{-\theta}\bigl(\mathbb{E}[(1+\kappa)^{-\theta}] - 1\bigr).

Using y\le 1 and condition (C),

\alpha - \beta y - \frac{\theta+1}{2}\sigma^2 \ge \delta_0 - \frac{\theta}{2}\sigma^2.

Choose \theta small enough so that \delta_0 - \frac{\theta}{2}\sigma^2 \ge \frac{\delta_0}{2} > 0. Then the first term is \le -\frac{\theta\delta_0}{2} x^{-\theta}. The jump term is bounded because \mathbb{E}[(1+\kappa)^{-\theta}] < \infty (for \theta small). So there exists C_1>0 such that

\mathcal{L}(x^{-\theta}) \le -\frac{\theta\delta_0}{2} x^{-\theta} + \theta\gamma x^{1-\theta} + C_1 x^{-\theta}. \tag{A.2}

Step 2: Generator of x^{p}.
For \varphi(x)=x^{p}:

\varphi'(x) = p x^{p-1}, \quad \varphi''(x) = p(p-1) x^{p-2}.

Then

\mathcal{L}(x^{p}) = p x^{p} (\alpha - \beta y - \gamma x) + \tfrac12 p(p-1) \sigma^2 x^{p} + \lambda_X x^{p}\bigl(\mathbb{E}[(1+\kappa)^{p}] - 1\bigr).

Grouping:

\mathcal{L}(x^{p}) = x^{p}\Bigl[ p(\alpha - \beta y) + \tfrac12 p(p-1)\sigma^2 + \lambda_X\bigl(\mathbb{E}[(1+\kappa)^{p}] - 1\bigr) \Bigr] - p\gamma x^{p+1}.

The term in brackets is bounded above by a constant C_2 (depending on p but finite because \mathbb{E}[(1+\kappa)^p]<\infty). Thus

\mathcal{L}(x^{p}) \le C_2 x^{p} - p\gamma x^{p+1}. \tag{A.3}

Step 3: Combined Lyapunov estimate.
Adding (A.2) and (A.3):

\mathcal{L}U(x) \le -\frac{\theta\delta_0}{2} x^{-\theta} + \theta\gamma x^{1-\theta} + C_1 x^{-\theta} + C_2 x^{p} - p\gamma x^{p+1}.

We now examine the asymptotic behaviour as x\to0^{+} and x\to\infty.

· As x\to0^+: The dominant negative term is -\frac{\theta\delta_0}{2} x^{-\theta} (since x^{-\theta} \gg x^{1-\theta} for small x). All other terms are bounded by a constant times x^{-\theta}, but we can adjust constants: for sufficiently small x, say x \le \varepsilon_0, we have

\theta\gamma x^{1-\theta} + C_1 x^{-\theta} \le \frac{\theta\delta_0}{4} x^{-\theta},

provided x is small enough that \theta\gamma x \le \frac{\theta\delta_0}{8} and C_1 \le \frac{\theta\delta_0}{8}. Actually C_1 is fixed; we can just choose \varepsilon_0 small enough so that \theta\gamma x^{1-\theta} \le \frac{\theta\delta_0}{4} x^{-\theta} (i.e., \theta\gamma x \le \frac{\theta\delta_0}{4}). Then

\mathcal{L}U(x) \le -\frac{\theta\delta_0}{4} x^{-\theta} + C_2 x^{p} - p\gamma x^{p+1} \quad\text{for } 0<x\le\varepsilon_0.

Since x^{p} and x^{p+1} are much smaller than x^{-\theta} near zero, there exists c_0>0 such that for all x\in(0,\varepsilon_0],

\mathcal{L}U(x) \le -c_0 U(x).

· As x\to\infty: The dominant term is -p\gamma x^{p+1} from the x^p part. The x^{1-\theta} term grows slower (1-\theta < p+1 for any p>0). All other terms are negligible compared to x^{p+1}. Thus there exist M>0 and c_\infty>0 such that for x\ge M,

\mathcal{L}U(x) \le -c_\infty U(x).

· Intermediate region: On the compact interval [\varepsilon_0, M], the generator \mathcal{L}U(x) is bounded above by some constant C_3 (since all terms are bounded). Define c_2 = C_3 + \sup_{x\in[\varepsilon_0,M]} (c_1 U(x)) where c_1 = \min(c_0, c_\infty). Then for all x\ge0,

\mathcal{L}U(x) \le -c_1 U(x) + c_2 \mathbf{1}_{[\varepsilon_0, M]}(x). \tag{A.4}

This is the Foster–Lyapunov drift condition (Down, Meyn, and Tweedie, 1995). Note that U(x)\ge 1 for all x>0 because x^{-\theta}\ge 1 for x\le 1 and x^p\ge 1 for x\ge 1; we can modify U outside [0,\infty) if needed.

Step 4: Moment bound.
Apply Itô’s formula to e^{c_1 t}U(X_t):

d(e^{c_1 t}U(X_t)) = e^{c_1 t}\bigl( \mathcal{L}U(X_t) + c_1 U(X_t) \bigr)dt + dM_t,

where M_t is a local martingale (Brownian plus compensated Poisson integrals). Using (A.4),

\mathcal{L}U(X_t) + c_1 U(X_t) \le c_2 \mathbf{1}_{[\varepsilon_0, M]}(X_t).

Take expectations, noting that the martingale term vanishes under suitable integrability (which can be justified by a standard stopping argument). Then

\mathbb{E}[e^{c_1 t}U(X_t)] \le U(X_0) + c_2 \int_0^t e^{c_1 s} \mathbb{P}(X_s\in[\varepsilon_0, M])\,ds.

Because the indicator is bounded by 1, the integral is \le \frac{c_2}{c_1}(e^{c_1 t}-1). Hence

\mathbb{E}[U(X_t)] \le U(X_0) e^{-c_1 t} + \frac{c_2}{c_1} (1-e^{-c_1 t}) \le U(X_0) + \frac{c_2}{c_1}.

Therefore \sup_{t\ge 0} \mathbb{E}[U(X_t)] < \infty.

Step 5: Stochastic permanence.
For any \varepsilon>0,

\mathbb{P}(X_t < \varepsilon) = \mathbb{P}(X_t^{-\theta} > \varepsilon^{-\theta}) \le \varepsilon^{\theta} \mathbb{E}[X_t^{-\theta}] \le \varepsilon^{\theta} \mathbb{E}[U(X_t)] \le \varepsilon^{\theta} M_0,

where M_0 = \sup_t \mathbb{E}[U(X_t)]. Thus for any \delta\in(0,1), choose \varepsilon_\delta = (\delta/M_0)^{1/\theta}. Then

\limsup_{t\to\infty} \mathbb{P}(X_t < \varepsilon_\delta) \le \delta,

and hence

\liminf_{t\to\infty} \mathbb{P}(X_t \ge \varepsilon_\delta) \ge 1-\delta.

This completes the proof of stochastic permanence. \square

