Appendix B: Year 2 – Affordance Optimal Control and Formal Verification

B.1 Controlled Dynamics and Assumptions

The colony’s cultural state evolves on a compact Riemannian manifold \mathcal{M} (a sublevel set of F) according to the controlled jump‑diffusion

dx_t = f(x_t, u_t)\,dt + \Sigma(x_t)\,d\mathbf{W}_t + \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \gamma(x_{t-}, u_t, z)\,\tilde{N}(dt,dz), \tag{B.1}

where \mathbf{W}_t is an m-dimensional Brownian motion, \tilde{N} is a compensated Poisson random measure with Lévy measure \nu satisfying \int (1\wedge|z|^2)\nu(dz)<\infty. The control process \{u_t\} takes values in a compact set \mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{R}^k and is progressively measurable.

Assumption H1. There exist constants L_f, C_f, L_\Sigma, C_\gamma, L_\gamma such that for all x,y\in\mathcal{M}, u\in\mathcal{U}:

· |f(x,u)-f(y,u)| \le L_f |x-y| and |f(x,u)| \le C_f(1+|x|);
· \|\Sigma(x)-\Sigma(y)\| \le L_\Sigma |x-y|;
· |\gamma(x,u,z)| \le C_\gamma (1+|x|)\rho(z) with \rho(z) = 1\wedge|z|, \int \rho(z)^2\nu(dz)<\infty;
· |\gamma(x,u,z)-\gamma(y,u,z)| \le L_\gamma |x-y|\rho(z);
· f(x,u)\in T_x\mathcal{M} and x+\gamma(x,u,z)\in\mathcal{M} for \nu-a.e. z (invariance).

Under H1, for any admissible control the SDE (B.1) has a unique strong solution with càdlàg paths and \mathbb{E}[\sup_{0\le t\le T}|x_t|^2]<\infty. Moreover, the process is Feller continuous: the transition kernel P_t(x,\cdot) is weakly continuous in x.

B.2 Dynamic Programming and the HJB Equation

Define the finite‑horizon discounted reward:

J(x,t; u_\cdot) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_t^T e^{-\rho(s-t)} R(x_s, u_s)\,ds + e^{-\rho(T-t)} \Psi(x_T) \;\middle|\; x_t=x \right],

where R:\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{U}\to\mathbb{R} is bounded continuous, and \Psi\in C_b(\mathcal{M}). The value function is V(x,t) = \sup_{u_\cdot} J(x,t; u_\cdot). The dynamic programming principle (DPP) states that for any stopping time \tau with values in [t,T],

V(x,t) = \sup_{u_\cdot} \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_t^\tau e^{-\rho(s-t)} R(x_s,u_s)ds + e^{-\rho(\tau-t)} V(x_\tau,\tau) \right].

Heuristically, assuming V smooth, Itô’s formula and the DPP lead to the HJB equation

0 = \max_{u\in\mathcal{U}} \Bigl\{ R(x,u) + \frac{\partial V}{\partial t} - \rho V + \mathcal{L}^u V \Bigr\}, \quad (x,t)\in\mathcal{M}\times[0,T),

V(x,T) = \Psi(x),

where \mathcal{L}^u is the generator of (B.1) acting on V(\cdot,t):

\mathcal{L}^u V = f(x,u)\cdot\nabla V + \tfrac12 \operatorname{Tr}(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2 V) + \int [V(x+\gamma(x,u,z)) - V - \nabla V\cdot\gamma]\,\nu(dz).

Since V is generally not smooth, we work with viscosity solutions.

Definition B.1 (Viscosity Solution). A continuous function V on \mathcal{M}\times[0,T] is a viscosity subsolution (resp. supersolution) of (B.2) if for every smooth test function \varphi such that V-\varphi has a local maximum (resp. minimum) at (x_0,t_0)\in\mathcal{M}\times[0,T),

-\frac{\partial\varphi}{\partial t}(x_0,t_0) + \rho V(x_0,t_0) - \max_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\bigl\{ R(x_0,u) + \mathcal{L}^u \varphi(x_0,t_0) \bigr\} \le 0 \quad (\text{resp. } \ge 0).

A viscosity solution is both a sub- and supersolution.

B.3 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Existence and Uniqueness)

Theorem 4.2. Under H1 and bounded continuous R,\Psi, the value function V is the unique continuous bounded viscosity solution of (B.2).

Proof.

1. Continuity. The Lipschitz estimates for the solution of (B.1) imply that for two initial states x_1,x_2,

\mathbb{E}\bigl[ \sup_{t\le T} |x_t^{x_1,u} - x_t^{x_2,u}| \bigr] \le C_T |x_1-x_2|,

uniformly in u. Because R and \Psi are bounded (and we may assume Lipschitz after a standard approximation), V is Lipschitz in x, hence jointly continuous in (x,t) by the DPP.

2. Viscosity property. We show V is a subsolution; the supersolution case is symmetric. Let \varphi be smooth and (x_0,t_0) a local maximum of V-\varphi with V(x_0,t_0)=\varphi(x_0,t_0). Suppose, for contradiction, that

-\varphi_t + \rho V - \max_{u}\{R+\mathcal{L}^u\varphi\} \ge \eta > 0 \quad \text{at } (x_0,t_0).

By continuity, there exists a small ball B around (x_0,t_0) where the inequality holds with \eta/2. For any admissible control, apply Itô’s formula to e^{-\rho t}\varphi(x_t,t) over a small time h until exiting B (stopped at \tau). One obtains

\varphi(x_0,t_0) \ge \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_0^{\tau} e^{-\rho s} R(x_s,u_s) ds + e^{-\rho \tau} V(x_\tau,t_0+\tau) \right] + \frac{\eta}{2} \mathbb{E}[\tau].

As h\to0, the DPP is violated. Hence the subsolution property holds.

3. Comparison principle (uniqueness). Let \underline{V} be a bounded subsolution and \overline{V} a bounded supersolution with \underline{V}(\cdot,T)\le \overline{V}(\cdot,T). We prove \underline{V}\le \overline{V} on \mathcal{M}\times[0,T].

Assume \sup(\underline{V}-\overline{V}) = m>0. For \alpha,\epsilon>0, consider

\Phi_{\alpha}(x,y,t) = \underline{V}(x,t) - \overline{V}(y,t) - \frac{\alpha}{2}|x-y|^2 - \frac{\epsilon}{2}(|x|^2+|y|^2).

By boundedness and continuity, a maximum exists at (x_\alpha,y_\alpha,t_\alpha). Standard arguments show that as \alpha\to\infty, |x_\alpha-y_\alpha|^2\alpha\to0 and (x_\alpha,y_\alpha,t_\alpha) converges to a point (\hat{x},\hat{x},\hat{t}) with \underline{V}(\hat{x},\hat{t})-\overline{V}(\hat{x},\hat{t}) = m.

Apply Ishii’s lemma for non‑local equations (see Barles & Imbert, 2008) to obtain matrices X,Y and vectors p_\alpha = \alpha(x_\alpha-y_\alpha) such that the viscosity inequalities hold:

-\partial_t \underline{V} + \rho\underline{V} - \max_u\{ R(x_\alpha,u) + \mathcal{L}^u \underline{V} \} \le 0,

-\partial_t \overline{V} + \rho\overline{V} - \max_u\{ R(y_\alpha,u) + \mathcal{L}^u \overline{V} \} \ge 0,

with appropriate spatial derivatives replaced by those of the test functions constructed from \Phi_\alpha. Subtracting yields

\rho(\underline{V}(x_\alpha) - \overline{V}(y_\alpha)) \le \max_u \{ R(x_\alpha,u) - R(y_\alpha,u) \} + \bigl[ \mathcal{L}^u \underline{V} - \mathcal{L}^u \overline{V} \bigr] + o(1).

The drift and diffusion terms are bounded by C\alpha|x_\alpha-y_\alpha|^2 (using Lipschitz properties and the matrix inequality from Ishii). The non‑local jump term is

\mathcal{I}^u = \int \Bigl( \underline{V}(x_\alpha+\gamma(x_\alpha,u,z)) - \underline{V}(x_\alpha) - p_\alpha\cdot\gamma(x_\alpha) \Bigr) \nu(dz) - \int \Bigl( \overline{V}(y_\alpha+\gamma(y_\alpha,u,z)) - \overline{V}(y_\alpha) - p_\alpha\cdot\gamma(y_\alpha) \Bigr) \nu(dz).

Using the fact that (x_\alpha,y_\alpha) is a maximum of \Phi_\alpha, we have

\underline{V}(x_\alpha+\gamma_x) - \overline{V}(y_\alpha+\gamma_y) \le \underline{V}(x_\alpha) - \overline{V}(y_\alpha) + \frac{\alpha}{2}( |x_\alpha-y_\alpha + \gamma_x - \gamma_y|^2 - |x_\alpha-y_\alpha|^2 ) + \epsilon(\dots).

Lipschitz continuity of \gamma gives |\gamma_x-\gamma_y|\le L_\gamma |x_\alpha-y_\alpha|\rho(z). Expanding the squared norm and using |x_\alpha-y_\alpha| = p_\alpha/\alpha, the additional term is bounded by C\alpha|x_\alpha-y_\alpha|^2\rho(z)^2. Integrating against \nu(dz) (recall \int\rho^2\nu(dz)<\infty) yields \mathcal{I}^u \le C\alpha|x_\alpha-y_\alpha|^2.

Therefore, as \alpha\to\infty, the right‑hand side converges to 0 while the left‑hand side converges to \rho m > 0, a contradiction. Hence m\le 0, and \underline{V}\le\overline{V}.

4. Existence. The value function V is a viscosity solution by Step 2. Uniqueness follows from the comparison principle. Alternatively, Perron’s method combined with the comparison principle yields existence. \square

B.4 Proof of Theorem 4.3 (ε‑Optimal Switched Policy)

Construction of the switched policy. Let \delta>0 and let \{\mathcal{R}_k\}_{k=1}^K be a finite partition of \mathcal{M} with \operatorname{diam}(\mathcal{R}_k)\le\delta and piecewise‑smooth boundaries. Choose reference points x_k\in\mathcal{R}_k. For each k, pick u_k^*\in\mathcal{U} that attains the supremum in the Hamiltonian at x_k (using a mollified version of V if necessary). Define the piecewise‑constant policy \pi_S(x) = u_k^* for x\in\mathcal{R}_k.

Semiconcavity and Lipschitz Hamiltonian. Under our assumptions, the value function V^* is semiconcave in x (see Fleming & Soner, Controlled Markov Processes and Viscosity Solutions, Ch. 9). This implies that the optimal Hamiltonian H^*(x) = \max_u \{ R(x,u) + \mathcal{L}^u V^* \} is Lipschitz continuous in x. In particular, there exists L_H such that

|H^*(x) - H^*(y)| \le L_H |x-y|.

The constant L_H can be explicitly bounded using the Lipschitz constants of f,\Sigma,\gamma,R, and the Lipschitz norm of V^* (which is known from the continuity estimates).

Performance bound. For any x\in\mathcal{R}_k,

H^*(x) - [R(x,\pi_S(x)) + \mathcal{L}^{\pi_S(x)} V^*(x)] \le H^*(x) - H^*(x_k) + \text{error from using } \pi_S(x) \text{ instead of optimal } u^*(x).

By definition of u_k^* and the Lipschitz property of H^*, this difference is at most L_H\delta. Applying the DPP along the trajectory under \pi_S and using Grönwall’s inequality, one obtains

V^*(x,0) - V^{\pi_S}(x,0) \le \frac{L_H\delta}{\rho}.

Set \epsilon = L_H\delta/\rho; then V^{\pi_S} \ge V^* - \epsilon. The efficiency loss is bounded by \Delta_{\max} = \max_u C_{\text{eff}}(u) - \min_u C_{\text{eff}}(u) < \infty because \mathcal{U} is compact. Since the switched policy does not enforce full automation, the loss is strictly positive but bounded. Agency safety is inherent because the policy only changes environmental parameters. \square

B.5 Predicate Abstraction and Soundness (Lemma 5.2)

Abstraction map. Let \mathcal{S} = \{s_1,\dots,s_M\} be a finite set whose elements correspond to Boolean combinations of the atomic predicates \mathcal{AP} (e.g., BelongState(h)). Define \alpha(x) = s iff x satisfies the predicate combination defining s. We discretize time with step \Delta>0 and consider the transition kernel

P^{\text{conc}}(x,u,A) = \mathbb{P}(x_\Delta \in A \mid x_0=x, u_0=u).

For each s,s'\in\mathcal{S} and u\in\mathcal{U}, define the interval transition probabilities

\check{P}_u(s,s') = \inf_{x\in\alpha^{-1}(s)} P^{\text{conc}}(x,u,\alpha^{-1}(s')), \quad
\hat{P}_u(s,s') = \sup_{x\in\alpha^{-1}(s)} P^{\text{conc}}(x,u,\alpha^{-1}(s')).

Definition B.2 (Probabilistic simulation). A relation \mathcal{R}\subseteq \mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{S} is a probabilistic simulation if for all (x,s)\in\mathcal{R} and all u\in\mathcal{U} there exists a weight function \Delta: \mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{S}\to[0,1] such that

· for all s', \sum_{s''} \Delta(s', s'') = P^{\text{conc}}(x,u,\alpha^{-1}(s'));
· for all s'', \sum_{s'} \Delta(s', s'') \in [\check{P}_u(s,s''), \hat{P}_u(s,s'')].

If \alpha itself is the projection of such a relation (i.e., (x,\alpha(x)) is in the simulation), we say the abstraction is a probabilistic simulation.

Lemma 5.2 (Soundness). Assume the concrete kernel is Feller continuous and the boundaries of all atomic predicates are Lipschitz (Assumption A2). If the abstraction is a probabilistic simulation, then for any PCTL formula \psi without nested probabilistic operators,

\mathcal{I} \models_{\text{lower}} \psi \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \text{concrete system satisfies } \psi,

where \mathcal{I} \models_{\text{lower}} \psi means that the minimum probability over all resolutions of the interval uncertainty satisfies the threshold.

Proof sketch. We prove by structural induction. The base case of atomic propositions holds because the partition respects their truth values. For a formula \mathbb{P}_{\ge 1-\delta}[\lozenge^{\le T} \Phi], suppose the IMDP model checker confirms that the lower bound probability is at least 1-\delta. This means that for the best choice of transition probabilities within the intervals, the probability of reaching a state satisfying \Phi within T steps is \ge 1-\delta. Because the concrete transition probabilities lie inside the intervals, a coupling argument shows that the concrete probability is at least the IMDP lower bound. The coupling is built stepwise using the weight functions \Delta; the inductive hypothesis ensures that reaching a \Phi-state in the IMDP implies reaching a state whose concrete counterpart satisfies \Phi. Thus the concrete system meets the specification. The Lipschitz boundary condition ensures that as the partition is refined, the interval widths go to zero, guaranteeing that any desired precision can be achieved. A full formal proof can be found in the literature on abstraction for stochastic hybrid systems (e.g., Baier & Katoen, Principles of Model Checking, Ch. 10). \square

B.6 Lean 4 Formalization Outline

The mechanisation targets the 50‑agent model. Because the state space of 50 agents with individual engagement levels is enormous, we use a counting abstraction: the state of the IMDP is a tuple (c_0,c_1,\dots,c_4) where c_i is the number of agents in engagement level i. By exchangeability of agents under the affordance policy (identical probabilistic response), the concrete dynamics project exactly onto this counting process. The number of abstract states is the number of weak compositions of 50 into 5 parts: \binom{50+5-1}{5-1} = 316,251. Further symmetry reduction and interval merging reduce this to \sim 5000 states.

The Lean formalization will:

1. Define the counting abstraction as a function \alpha: \mathcal{M}_{\text{conc}} \to \mathcal{S} and prove that it is a probabilistic simulation using the symmetry.
2. Formalize PCTL syntax and semantics, and implement a fixed-point model checking algorithm for IMDPs using value iteration with interval arithmetic.
3. Prove the algorithm correct: if it returns true, then the lower satisfaction probability exceeds the required bound.
4. Verify the CEGAR loop: given a spurious counterexample, refine the partition along predicate boundaries; prove termination because the boundaries are Lipschitz.

The soundness theorem (Lemma 5.2) will be fully proven within Lean for the finite-state case, establishing the correctness of the entire verification pipeline for the 50‑agent example.



